1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,940
Far away from us, around distant stars,

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:06,180
scientists have already found thousands of these exoplanets.

3
00:00:07,260 --> 00:00:09,580
Roman will look toward the center of our own Milky Way.

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:13,200
Where it can see literally hundreds of thousands of stars

5
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:15,200
in a single image at once.

6
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:18,140
We know that most stars have planets.

7
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:20,680
Which means Roman will detect thousands

8
00:00:20,680 --> 00:00:22,660
more exoplanets out there.

9
00:00:23,160 --> 00:00:25,020
With a cutting-edge piece of technology,

10
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:28,240
Roman will see far more detail of some planets

11
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:29,880
than we've ever seen before.

12
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:32,680
These planets are so incredibly faint

13
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:36,280
that you get one photon, one particle of light.

14
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:39,300
Arriving at our camera from one of those planets

15
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:42,160
once every few seconds or maybe even once every minute.

16
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:44,900
So we are getting one little smudge

17
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:47,420
